C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      HW4 Problem 2
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      Shelving units are distributed from manufacturers to suppliers to recipients. Data is as follows:
       
      Maximum number of shelves manufacturers can produce:
      Arnold: 75
      Supershelf: 75
       
      Minimum number of shelves needed at recipients:
      Zrox: 50
      Hewes: 60
      Rock-Wright: 40
       
      Cost to ship from manufacturer to supplier
                       Thomas    Wash-Burn  
       Arnold             5          8     
       Supershelf         7          4
       
      Cost to ship from supplier to recipient
                        Zrox        Hewes     Rock-Wright
      Thomas              1           5           8
      Wash-Burn           3           4           4
       
                      Determine the optimal path for delivery that will minimize cost of shipping.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      Izzy Hinton
      isabel.hinton@usu.edu
      October 04, 2026
  37   
  38  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  39  SETS man manufacturers /Arnold, Supershelf/
  40       sup suppliers /Thomas, Wash-Burn/
  41       recip recipients /Zrox, Hewes, Rock-Wright/;
  42   
  43  * 2. DEFINE input data
  44   
  45  TABLE MantoSupCosts(man,sup) Costs to ship from each manufacturer to each supplier objective function coefficients
  46                   Thomas    Wash-Burn
  47   Arnold             5          8
  48   Supershelf         7          4;
  49   
  50  TABLE SuptoRecipCosts(sup, recip) Costs to ship from each supplier to each recipient objective function coeffiecients
  51                    Zrox        Hewes     Rock-Wright
  52  Thomas              1           5           8
  53  Wash-Burn           3           4           4;
  54   
  55  Parameters mintorecips(recip) minimum number of shelves that need to be delivered to each recipient
  56      /Zrox   50,
  57      Hewes   60
  58      Rock-Wright 40/;
  59   
  60  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  61  VARIABLES X(man,sup) shelves shipped from a manufacturer to a supplier (Number)
  62            Y(sup, recip) shelves shipped from a supplier to a recipient (Number)
  63            VCOST  total cost ($);
  64   
  65  * Non-negativity constraints
  66  POSITIVE VARIABLES X, Y;
  67   
  68  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  69  EQUATIONS
  70     COST Total cost ($) and objective function value
  71     MAN_CONSTRAIN(man) Constraints on manufacturing supplies
  72     SUP_CONSTRAIN(sup) Constraints on what suppliers are able to ship to recipients
  73     RECIP_CONSTRAIN(recip) Constraints on minimum shelves that need to be delivered to recipients;
  74   
  75  COST..                   VCOST =E= SUM((man,sup), MantoSupCosts(man,sup)*X(man,sup)) + SUM((sup, recip), SuptoRecipCosts(sup, recip)*Y(sup, recip));
  76  MAN_CONSTRAIN(man)..     Sum(sup, X(man, sup)) =L= 75;
  77  SUP_CONSTRAIN(sup)..     Sum(man, X(man, sup)) =G= Sum(recip, Y(sup,recip));
  78  RECIP_CONSTRAIN(recip).. Sum(sup, Y(sup, recip)) =G= mintorecips(recip);
  79   
  80   
  81  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  82  MODEL DELIVERING /ALL/;
  83  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  84  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  85   
  86   
  87  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  88  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  89  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  90  SOLVE DELIVERING USING LP MINIMIZING VCOST;
  91   
  92   
  93  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


